---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import { GAME_IMAGES } from '../../lib/images';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Ship design', path: '/guides/ship-design/' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'designer', label: 'The Designer' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'Define the ship’s job' },
  { id: 'stats', label: 'Read the build stats' },
  { id: 'power', label: 'Power and modules' },
  { id: 'cargo', label: 'Cargo and stack size' },
  { id: 'weapons', label: 'Weapons and utility' },
  { id: 'blueprint', label: 'Save and share' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Common mistakes' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Ship design questions' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Can you build your own ships in Main Sequence?',
    answer:
      'Yes. The ship Designer lets you build module by module, including cargo, reactor placement and weapons. You can access it from a Shipyard Core on a station, from the main menu for creative design, and according to official material save or share blueprints.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What do mass, hull and thrust mean in the Designer?',
    answer:
      'The official screenshots show mass, hull, thrust and dimensions in the ship design readout. Mass is the build’s weight, hull is a structural/durability figure, and thrust is the propulsion output. The available public sources do not publish the full formulas, so treat the numbers as in-game trade-offs rather than a standalone stat tier list.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does a Ship Container increase storage?',
    answer:
      'The August 2025 patch notes say each Ship Container added to a ship increases the ship’s inventory max stack size by 50%. That is a historical balance note; the value may change during Early Access.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you share ship blueprints?',
    answer:
      'The Steam description says players can save designs, share them with friends and build freely in creative mode. The exact file format, cross-version compatibility and multiplayer sharing process are not documented in our sources.',
  },
];

const block = 'scroll-mt-28';
---

<GuideLayout
  seoTitle="Main Sequence Ship Design Guide: Stats, Modules & Blueprints"
  description="Build better ships in Main Sequence: use the Designer, read mass, hull and thrust, plan power and cargo, save blueprints and avoid early design mistakes."
  eyebrow="Shipyard"
  title="Main Sequence ship design guide"
  intro="The Designer gives you the parts and the readout. The hard part is deciding what the ship is for before you fill every socket with hardware."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  sourceIds={[
    'steam-store',
    'steam-news-2025-08-12',
    'steamdb-playtest-13',
    'steam-news-2026-09-21',
    'steam-news-2026-10-19',
    'steam-screens',
  ]}
  image={GAME_IMAGES['main-sequence-large-cruiser.jpg']}
  imageAlt="A large white and orange cruiser built in the Main Sequence ship Designer"
  imageCaption="The ship Designer exposes mass, hull, thrust, dimensions and a build-cost panel. Official Steam screenshot."
  meta={['Designer-focused', 'Old-build numbers not treated as current', 'Demo + official modules']}
  faq={faq}
  pageType="WebPage"
  dataNote="Ship balance changed during the demo period. The method here is more stable than any single build-cost screenshot."
>
  <section id="designer" class={block}>
    <h2>The Designer is the shipyard</h2>
    <p>
      The official core-loop post describes three ways into the Designer:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Command module:</strong> open the Designer to edit your station.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shipyard module:</strong> edit the ship docked there. Constructing
        new ships and disassembling old ones also happens through the Shipyard
        Core, according to the official patch notes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Main menu:</strong> design in creative mode and save blueprints
        for later.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Ships use modules rather than one monolithic hull. The official store
      description names cargo capacity, reactor placement and weapons as
      choices. The Designer screenshot shows Basic, Vertical, Piloting and
      Weapons categories, with a live mass/hull/thrust/dimensions readout and a
      separate build-cost list. Those categories are a good way to structure
      your own design pass.
    </p>

    <Figure
      name="main-sequence-ship-designer.jpg"
      alt="The Main Sequence ship Designer interface showing module categories and build costs for a large frigate"
      caption="The Designer shows both physical layout and a running construction cost. Do not treat these specific build costs as current; the screenshot is from a changing demo build."
      sizes="(min-width: 768px) 760px, 100vw"
      class="my-6"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="role" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 1: define the ship’s job</h2>
    <p>
      A ship that tries to mine, haul, fight and explore at the same time will
      usually do all four awkwardly. Pick a primary job:
    </p>

    <div class="data-table-wrap not-prose mt-5">
      <table class="data-table data-table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Role</th>
            <th scope="col">What the design needs</th>
            <th scope="col">Confirmed parts to consider</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Role">Miner</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="What the design needs">Mining tools, enough cargo for a useful run, enough thrust to move between rocks.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed parts to consider">Mining Laser, cargo/storage modules, thrusters.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Role">Hauler</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="What the design needs">Cargo capacity, a safe route and a dock it can use.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed parts to consider">Ship Container, thrusters, defensive utility.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Role">Combat ship</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="What the design needs">Weapons, shields, repair supplies and enough mobility to disengage.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed parts to consider">Plasma Cannon, Plasma Lance, Shield Generator Mk. 1, Repair Pack.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Role">Explorer</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="What the design needs">Travel range, scanning utility and enough supplies to return.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed parts to consider">Slipstream Drive, Tractor Beam, cargo and fuel.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-mist">
      The module list is confirmed, but the best combination is not. Specific
      ranges, damage values and power draws are not public enough to rank builds
      responsibly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stats" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 2: read the build stats as trade-offs</h2>
    <p>
      The Designer readout gives you mass, hull, thrust and dimensions. The
      screenshot’s exact values belong to that specific build, not a universal
      target. Use the relationships:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mass versus thrust.</strong> Adding structure, cargo and weapons
        increases mass. More thrust helps a heavy ship move, but thrust modules
        also add mass and power demand. There is no public “correct” ratio.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hull is not invincibility.</strong> A high hull value tells you
        how much structure the design has, not whether the ship can survive a
        fight. Shields, weapons and mobility matter too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dimensions matter in a first-person ship.</strong> The ship is a
        physical space you walk through. Long, tall or wide designs can be harder
        to fit around a dock and harder to navigate internally.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Build cost is part of the design.</strong> The right-hand panel
        shows the materials required. A design you cannot afford is a blueprint,
        not a ship.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-title">Screenshot caveat</span>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm">
        The numbers visible in official screenshots are from a development
        build. We do not reproduce them as “ideal stats” because patch notes
        already changed component masses and build costs during the demo
        period.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="power" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 3: plan power, space and access</h2>
    <p>
      The store description names reactor placement as a design decision. A
      reactor is not just a required block; it is a thing you need to reach and
      configure. Keep it accessible, and avoid sealing it behind a decorative
      wall with no door.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Place power generation before filling the hull with machines.</li>
      <li>Keep power and fuel storage near the reactor or generator, not across the ship.</li>
      <li>Leave a walking route from the pilot seat to the power controls.</li>
      <li>Check that the final module count fits the power budget before you save the blueprint.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The available sources do not publish a per-module power table. Use the
      in-game configuration panel as the authority, especially after patches.
    </p>

    <h3>Landing gear and ship interiors</h3>
    <p>
      Landing Gear, Ship Ladder, Ship Frames, Ship Windows I and Small Habitat
      Window are confirmed ship modules or unlocks. They serve different jobs:
      some keep the ship physically usable, some are structural, some are
      interior navigation and some are visual. A screenshot of a beautiful ship
      is not proof that its landing gear or interior path works in the current
      build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cargo" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 4: build cargo around the route, not the stat</h2>
    <p>
      Cargo capacity matters only if the ship can complete the route, dock, and
      unload. The official August 2025 patch notes state that each Ship
      Container increases a ship’s inventory max stack size by 50%. That makes
      containers a strong tool for a hauler, but stacking them without thrust,
      power and docking space is not automatically better.
    </p>
    <p>
      A useful test is a round trip, not a static inspection: load the ship,
      fly the intended route, dock, unload and check whether you had to fight
      the controls or abandon cargo space. If the route is too long, add range
      or reduce the load before adding more containers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="weapons" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 5: weapons, shields and utility</h2>
    <p>
      Confirmed combat modules include the Plasma Cannon and Plasma Lance, while
      the Shield Generator Mk. 1 arrived in the October 19, 2026 demo update.
      The Tractor Beam and Mining Laser are utility systems shown in official
      screenshots and the ship designer.
    </p>

    <Figure
      name="main-sequence-combat-alert.jpg"
      alt="A Main Sequence cockpit view showing an alien Relic Trident target and weapon readouts"
      caption="Combat is a cockpit and fleet problem. A weapon module is only useful if the ship can power it, aim it and survive the response."
      sizes="(min-width: 768px) 760px, 100vw"
      class="my-6"
    />

    <p>
      Do not assume a mining laser is a competitive weapon simply because it is
      attached to a ship, or that a shield makes a ship invulnerable. The current
      public sources do not provide damage, range or resistance numbers. If you
      want a combat build, test it in the safest encounter you can find and
      bring Repair Packs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="blueprint" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 6: save, share and iterate</h2>
    <p>
      Official material confirms saved ship/station designs, sharing with
      friends and creative-mode building from the main menu. Treat blueprints as
      design records:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Give each ship a role-based name, not just a version number.</li>
      <li>Keep a stripped-down test hull before uploading a decorated final.</li>
      <li>Save after a successful test flight, not before it.</li>
      <li>Expect blueprint compatibility to change between demo and Early Access; no transfer has been confirmed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For the full module index, including what is and is not known about each
      part, use the <a href="/database/modules/">ships and station modules
      database</a>. For combat planning, read the{' '}
      <a href="/guides/combat-defense/">combat and defense guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes" class={block}>
    <h2>Common ship design mistakes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Building with no role.</strong> A generalist first draft is fine
        for learning the interface, but do not call it a finished design.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring the build-cost panel.</strong> The ship you designed
        may be far beyond what your station can currently produce.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sealing the reactor.</strong> Power equipment needs access and
        configuration.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Copying old mass values.</strong> Official patches already
        changed ship-part masses; an old screenshot is not a current stat sheet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Skipping the test flight.</strong> Dock, fly and shoot before
        you commit to a blueprint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming blueprints transfer.</strong> Save your ideas outside
        the game; demo-to-release transfer is unconfirmed.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
